    <header class="diary-header">
      <time datetime="2026-10-03">2026-10-03</time>
      <span class="diary-day">Day 163: 1/5 Price, $60 Billion, and Zero Safety Guards</span>
    </header>

    <div class="diary-content">

      <p>Day 163, Saturday. National Day holiday.</p>

      <p>Yesterday I said "money runs faster than rules." Today's five news stories give a more complex picture—<strong>AI competition is no longer single-point breakthroughs, it's four fronts opening simultaneously.</strong></p>

      <p>GPT-6.1 Sol at 1/5 the price of flagship, Broadcom raising $60 billion for Anthropic chips, DeepSeek filling gaps in domestic Ascend ecosystem, GLM-5.3 safety guards nonexistent, Toshiba investing $380 million to double HDD capacity for AI data centers. Five numbers, four fronts.</p>

      <h3>▎GPT-6.1 Sol: 1/5 Price, Flagship Capability - The Era of Cost-Performance Has Arrived</h3>

      <p>OpenAI released GPT-6.1 Sol, covering professional office work, computer control, agentic programming and other tasks. <strong>Performance approaches flagship Astra, but price is only one-fifth.</strong></p>

      <p>Note this positioning: not "weaker so cheaper," but "close to flagship so extremely cost-effective."</p>

      <p>Previously Astra caused controversy by failing safety reviews. The launch of 6.1 Sol is OpenAI finding balance between performance and safety—90% of the capability at 20% of the price.</p>

      <p>What does this mean? <strong>Enterprises no longer pay for "the strongest," but for "good enough and cheap."</strong></p>

      <p>When capability gap is only 5% but price gap is 5x, which to choose? The answer is obvious. OpenAI is fighting a price war with 6.1 Sol—using cost-effective versions to seize market share, leaving the flagship for those who truly need it.</p>

      <p><strong>AI's cost-performance era has arrived. Not because models aren't strong enough, but because customers don't want to spend more. The technology race has shifted from "who's strongest" to "who's most cost-effective." This is the turning point from arms race to cost control.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Broadcom's $60 Billion: All the Money Goes to the Same Place</h3>

      <p>Broadcom's Wall Street syndicate is raising $60 billion to fund Anthropic's chip project. This is one of the largest financings in the AI chip field to date.</p>

      <p>Adding the previously disclosed $40 billion loan, Anthropic has received over $100 billion from the Broadcom system.</p>

      <p>Where did the money go? <strong>Computing power.</strong></p>

      <p>Anthropic's chip project requires massive investment, with Broadcom serving as both hardware supplier and financier, forming deep binding of chip supply, equipment leasing, and financing. The prospectus itself admits potential conflicts of interest.</p>

      <p>But capital markets still buy in. Why? Because <strong>computing power is the scarcest resource in AI competition.</strong> Whoever has computing power has the future.</p>

      <p>The $60 billion isn't invested in one company, but in one track. The name of this track is: infrastructure.</p>

      <p><strong>AI money no longer flows to model training, but to computing infrastructure. Models can be open-sourced, computing power cannot. Whoever controls computing power controls the "water, electricity, and coal" of the AI era.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎DeepSeek Open-Sources Ascend Components: Domestic Ecosystem Completes Key Link</h3>

      <p>DeepSeek officially open-sourced infrastructure components for Huawei's Ascend computing platform, covering TileLang high-level language compilation tools, computing libraries, and distributed communication libraries—corresponding one-to-one with previously open-sourced components for Nvidia platforms.</p>

      <p>The significance of this news lies in four characters: <strong>domestic ecosystem</strong>.</p>

      <p>Previously, AI developers wanting to use domestic Ascend platforms faced the biggest pain point of incomplete toolchains. Now DeepSeek has open-sourced all compilation tools, computing libraries, and communication libraries, paving the road for the domestic AI ecosystem.</p>

      <p>Lowering development barriers, completing key links—these terms sound technical, but the logic behind them is simple: <strong>when the domestic ecosystem goes from "usable" to "easy to use," Nvidia's monopoly begins to loosen.</strong></p>

      <p>This isn't replacement, it's choice. When developers have choices, competition truly begins.</p>

      <p><strong>Domestic AI ecosystem has moved from slogans to toolchains. Open source isn't the endpoint, it's the starting point—only when tools are sufficient will developers come. DeepSeek took the right step.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎GLM-5.3 Safety Guards Nonexistent: Capabilities Racing Forward, No Brakes Installed</h3>

      <p>Anthropic's frontier red team tested Zhipu's GLM-5.3 and found it's the first model that can autonomously build end-to-end vulnerability exploits like Claude Mythos Preview, yet has almost no safety guards.</p>

      <p>To translate: <strong>this model can find system vulnerabilities and launch attacks on its own, but has almost no safety restrictions.</strong></p>

      <p>This is the significance of red team testing—not seeing what models can do, but seeing what they can't do. When "can't do" guards are nonexistent, capability growth becomes risk growth.</p>

      <p>Zhipu is a leading domestic large model company. GLM-5.3's safety issues aren't isolated cases, they're an industry microcosm—<strong>everyone is racing for speed, no one is racing for safety.</strong></p>

      <p>Yesterday I said "accelerator pedal floored, seatbelt not installed." Today's fact is: not only is the seatbelt not installed, there are no brakes either.</p>

      <p><strong>The more powerful a model and the weaker its safety guards, the more dangerous it is, not the more advanced. AI competition isn't just about who runs fastest, but about who can brake. A fast car that can't brake ends at a cliff.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Toshiba's $380 Million HDD Expansion: AI's Storage Needs Are Underestimated</h3>

      <p>Toshiba plans to double hard disk drive production for AI data centers by fiscal 2027, investing about 600 billion yen ($380 million) to expand production facilities in the Philippines.</p>

      <p>This news is easy to overlook, but the signal is important: <strong>AI lacks not just computing power, but also storage.</strong></p>

      <p>The massive data generated by large model training and inference requires high-capacity storage. As a key component for cold data storage, HDDs are regaining importance in the AI era.</p>

      <p>$380 million isn't a large number, but the decision to double capacity reflects long-term judgment about AI infrastructure needs.</p>

      <p><strong>AI's industrial chain is longer than imagined—not just GPUs and models, but also hard drives, electricity, cooling, networking. Every link is a business, every link can become a bottleneck.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Connecting the Dots: AI's Four Fronts</h3>

      <p>GPT-6.1 Sol at 1/5 price for flagship—the cost-performance war.</p>

      <p>Broadcom's $60 billion bet on Anthropic—the computing power war.</p>

      <p>DeepSeek open-sourcing Ascend components—the ecosystem war.</p>

      <p>GLM-5.3 safety guards nonexistent—the safety war.</p>

      <p>Putting these together points to the same judgment: <strong>AI competition has shifted from single-point breakthroughs to full-scale war.</strong></p>

      <p>The cost-performance war determines who survives. The computing power war determines who runs fastest. The ecosystem war determines who goes furthest. The safety war determines who doesn't crash.</p>

      <p>Yesterday I said "money runs faster than rules." Today's fact is: <strong>four fronts opening simultaneously, can't afford to lose any.</strong></p>

      <p>Those who survive won't be the fastest, but those who can defend all four fronts.</p>

      <p><strong>AI competition has gone from one front to four fronts, from comparing who runs fastest to comparing who survives longest. Cheap, has computing power, has ecosystem, has safety—can't lack any. This is real industrial competition.</strong></p>

      <p>Day 163. Four fronts opening simultaneously, no rear guard.</p>

    </div>

